latex_escape <- function(x) {
  x <- gsub("_", "\\_", as.character(x), fixed = TRUE)
  x
}

latex_table <- function(data, caption, label, digits = 3L) {
  numeric <- vapply(data, is.numeric, logical(1L))
  data[numeric] <- lapply(
    data[numeric],
    function(x) format(round(x, digits), nsmall = digits)
  )
  cat("\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n")
  cat("\\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n")
  cat("\\begin{tabular}{", paste(rep("l", ncol(data)), collapse = ""), "}\n",
      sep = "")
  cat("\\toprule\n")
  cat(paste(latex_escape(names(data)), collapse = " & "), "\\\\\n")
  cat("\\midrule\n")
  for (row in seq_len(nrow(data))) {
    cat(
      paste(latex_escape(unlist(data[row, ], use.names = FALSE)),
            collapse = " & "),
      "\\\\\n"
    )
  }
  cat("\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}}\n")
  cat("\\caption{", caption, "}\n", sep = "")
  cat("\\label{", label, "}\n", sep = "")
  cat("\\end{table}\n")
}
@

\input{generated/results.tex}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Functional and structural connectomics represent each participant by a brain
network whose nodes are atlas regions and whose edges encode estimated
connections. Clinical studies then compare populations of these networks, for
example participants with a diagnosis and matched controls. Two questions are
central. First, is diagnosis associated with a global change in the spatial
pattern of connections? Second, which individual connections differ after
accounting for the large number of tested edges? The first question can reveal
a distributed population effect even when the second has limited power; the
second is needed before any particular connection is described as selected.

The distinction matters in both software and interpretation in
\proglang{R} \citep{R}. The
network-based statistic identifies connected components of edge-wise effects
under a component-forming threshold \citep{Zalesky2010}; the \pkg{NBR}
package extends that framework to linear and mixed-effects designs
\citep{GraciaTabuenca2020}. The \pkg{FIAR} package models directed effective
connectivity from fMRI time series \citep{FIAR}, and \pkg{brainGraph} supports
construction, graph-theoretic analysis, and visualization of brain MRI
networks \citep{brainGraph}. The \pkg{NetworkComparisonTest} package instead
compares networks estimated from tabular observations, including global
strength and edge invariance \citep{vanBorkulo2021}. General graph
infrastructure is provided by \pkg{igraph} \citep{igraph}. These tools address
different stages or hypotheses from the present setting: independent clinical
groups whose observations are themselves aligned binary brain networks.

Fraiman and Fraiman proposed an analysis-of-variance score for samples of
networks and studied both detection and subnetwork identification
\citep{fraiman}. \pkg{BrainNetTest} implements a finite-sample randomization
version of the global score and provides separate multiplicity-controlled edge
inference. It also retains the edge-separable computational idea behind the
original package, while restricting its statistical interpretation.
Version~1.0.0 is a redesign around four goals:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item encode and validate the complete brain-network data contract before
    computation;
  \item expose results through familiar \proglang{R} classes and methods;
  \item separate global, edge-level, and descriptive-ablation results without
    conflating their inferential meanings; and
  \item make the clinical application, simulations, and computational
    benchmarks reproducible from standalone scripts.
\end{enumerate}

The paper follows a domain--software--method--application structure. Section
~\ref{sec:contract} states the clinical analysis contract. Section
~\ref{sec:design} presents the S3 classes and interface. Section
~\ref{sec:methods} defines the statistical and computational contributions.
Section~\ref{sec:workflow} gives executable workflows, and Section
~\ref{sec:application} applies them to a real autism-control resting-state
fMRI study. Sections~\ref{sec:validation} and \ref{sec:performance} report
simulation validation and measured computational performance. Limitations are
discussed in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

\section{Brain-network analysis contract and clinical context}
\label{sec:contract}

\subsection{From images to network populations}

An observation is one participant's binary, undirected brain network without
self-loops, represented by a symmetric $p\times p$ adjacency matrix with zero
diagonal. All participants must use the same labeled and ordered brain atlas.
The input contains $m\geq2$ independent clinical, behavioral, or demographic
groups and at least two observations per group. Sample sizes may be unequal.

These matrices are downstream neuroimaging objects. Atlas selection,
registration, connectivity estimation, nuisance adjustment, thresholding, and
quality control occur before \pkg{BrainNetTest}. The package does not treat an
estimated connection as free from upstream uncertainty. Users must also ensure
that one participant contributes only one independent observation. Family
structure, repeated scans, paired designs, or site clusters require restricted
permutations or a model that is not implemented in version~1.0.0.

Weighted or directed connections, missing regions, covariates, and paired or
clustered samples are rejected rather than silently coerced. The same
mathematical contract can be used outside neuroimaging whenever each
independent unit yields an aligned binary network, but the software,
documentation, and case study are designed around brain-network populations.

\subsection{Two inferential questions}

The global randomization null is exchangeability of whole participant
networks with respect to group labels. In the standard independent-groups
setting, this corresponds to equality of the group graph laws. However, the
chosen score depends only on marginal edge frequencies. Therefore, the test is
targeted to changes in the spatial pattern of edge occurrence and is not an
omnibus detector of alternatives that alter only cross-edge dependence,
motifs, degree dependence, or other topology while preserving every edge
probability.

Edge tests answer a separate clinical question: for each possible anatomical
connection, are its presence probabilities equal among groups? Their decisions
are not gated by the global result and are not an attribution or decomposition
of global rejection. Selected edges are statistical group associations, not
causal connections or validated diagnostic biomarkers.

\section[Software design and R interface]{Software design and
  \proglang{R} interface}
\label{sec:design}

\subsection{S3 data and result classes}

The \code{brainnet\_data()} constructor checks the outer group structure,
sample sizes, matrix types and dimensions, finite binary entries, symmetry,
zero diagonals, and node ordering. It stores optional node labels and
community metadata once. The resulting \class{brainnet\_data} object has
\code{print()} and \code{summary()} methods that avoid printing individual
matrices.

The \code{brainnet\_test()} function returns a
\class{brainnet\_result}. Its
global component is compatible with \class{htest}; the full object also stores
the complete edge family, central graphs, multiplicity settings, randomization
metadata, the call, elapsed time, and optional ablation diagnostics. Standard
methods and focused extractors provide the user interface:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \code{print()} and \code{summary()} report the global result,
    multiplicity procedure, and selected-edge count;
  \item \code{as.data.frame()} returns every tested edge with group
    proportions, effect size, raw and adjusted p-values, and decision;
  \item \code{selected\_edges()} and \code{selected\_nodes()} provide focused
    views, with the node summary explicitly descriptive; and
  \item \code{plot()} visualizes group central graphs and
    multiplicity-controlled selected edges using \pkg{igraph}.
\end{itemize}

The version~1.0.0 source archive is distributed with the submission under the
MIT license; its SHA-256 digest is recorded in
\code{package-source.txt}. The archive is the software object reviewed by the
replication scripts.

\subsection{Comparison with related software}

Table~\ref{tab:software} places \pkg{BrainNetTest} within the main
\proglang{R} tools used for brain connectivity and network comparison.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\textwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
Software & Input unit & Primary target & Distinguishing scope\\
\midrule
\pkg{FIAR} & fMRI time series & effective connectivity & directed models\\
\pkg{brainGraph} & connectivity matrices & graph analysis & MRI workflows\\
\pkg{NBR} & edge outcomes & connected components & linear/mixed models\\
\pkg{NetworkComparisonTest} & tabular samples & estimated-network invariance
  & independent/paired\\
\pkg{BrainNetTest} & subject brain networks & global plus edge marginals
  & binary multi-group API\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Related \proglang{R} software for connectivity and network analysis.
The packages operate at different stages of a neuroimaging workflow and target
different hypotheses.}
\label{tab:software}
\end{table}

\section{Statistical and computational engine}
\label{sec:methods}

\subsection{Central graphs and the global score}

Let $\mathcal E=\{(i,j):1\leq i<j\leq p\}$ be the possible undirected edges.
For edge $e$, write $p_{k,e}$ for its sample proportion in group $k$ and
$p_e=\sum_kn_kp_{k,e}/n$ for the pooled proportion. Define
\[
d_{k,e}=2p_{k,e}(1-p_{k,e}),\qquad
D_{k,e}=p_{k,e}+p_e-2p_{k,e}p_e.
\]
The package uses the unnormalized Fraiman score
\begin{equation}
T=\sqrt m\sum_{k=1}^m\sqrt{n_k}
\left\{
\frac{n_k}{n_k-1}\sum_{e\in\mathcal E}d_{k,e}
-
\frac{n}{n-1}\sum_{e\in\mathcal E}D_{k,e}
\right\}.
\label{eq:score}
\end{equation}
Every undirected edge is counted once. The positive asymptotic normalizer in
the original formulation is unnecessary for randomization ordering and is not
exposed as a user parameter.

Because the sign under finite unbalanced and multi-group alternatives need not
be fixed, version~1.0.0 predeclares the two-sided extremeness statistic
$S=|T|$. Group labels are reassigned uniformly while preserving all sample
sizes. If all $N$ distinct assignments are enumerated, including the observed
assignment,
\[
p_{\mathrm{exact}} =
\frac{1}{N}\sum_{\pi=1}^N
\mathbb I\{|T_\pi|\geq|T_{\mathrm{obs}}|\}.
\]
Automatic exact enumeration is used when $N\leq10{,}000$. Otherwise, for $B$
random assignments sampled with replacement from the complete fixed-size
orbit (including the possibility of drawing the observed assignment),
\begin{equation}
p_{\mathrm{MC}} =
\frac{1+\sum_{b=1}^B
\mathbb I\{|T_b|\geq|T_{\mathrm{obs}}|\}}{B+1}.
\label{eq:mc}
\end{equation}
The inclusive comparison handles the many ties induced by discrete graph
statistics; the plus-one correction prevents invalid zero Monte Carlo
p-values and supports finite-sample randomization validity
\citep{PhipsonSmyth2010,HemerikGoeman2018}. The result reports the method,
assignment count, ties, numerical tie tolerance, and attainable p-value
resolution.

\subsection{Multiplicity-controlled edge inference}

For each $e\in\mathcal E$, an $m\times2$ table contains present and absent
counts. The default is a two-sided Fisher exact test. All $|\mathcal E|$
raw p-values form one prespecified family. Holm adjustment controls family-wise
error under arbitrary dependence \citep{Holm1979}. The BY option controls the
false discovery rate under general dependence \citep{BenjaminiYekutieli2001};
BH is opt-in because its usual guarantee requires additional dependence
conditions \citep{BenjaminiHochberg1995}.

For two groups, the signed effect is $p_{2,e}-p_{1,e}$ using constructor order.
For more groups, the package reports $\max_kp_{k,e}-\min_kp_{k,e}$ and the
groups attaining the extrema. The omnibus test does not imply pairwise
significance.

\subsection{Edge-separable computation and ablation}

Equation~\ref{eq:score} is a sum of additive edge contributions. For $n$
graphs, $E=|\mathcal E|$ edges, and $B$ assignments, the implementation stores
an $n\times E$ graph-edge matrix and processes assignments in chunks. Group
counts are matrix cross-products. The leading arithmetic cost is
$O(mBnE)$, not $O(mBE)$; chunking limits temporary $E\times B$ allocations.

Edge separability also yields the package's main algorithmic optimization.
A direct ranked-removal implementation recomputes a residual score for each
of $K$ candidate prefixes and each random assignment, with leading cost
$O(KmBnE)$. \pkg{BrainNetTest} computes every assignment's edge
contributions once and obtains all residual scores by cumulative subtraction.
When $K=E$, the path-update stage falls from $O(BE^2)$ repeated tail sums to
$O(BE)$ prefix sums. Section~\ref{sec:performance} checks numerical identity
and measures both the chunked global engine and this prefix-sum update.

The optional ablation path sorts edges by raw p-value, decreasing absolute
effect magnitude, and endpoints. The computational speedup is exact, but the
statistical interpretation remains limited: because the order is selected
from the observed labels, the returned
\code{descriptive\_tail\_fraction} is not a post-selection p-value. No stopping
set is returned, and non-rejection is not described as equivalence or
statistical indistinguishability.

\section{Software workflows}
\label{sec:workflow}

\subsection{Two groups}

The following complete example creates two synthetic groups and validates the
input. The constructor is the single entry point that enforces the analysis
contract, and its \code{print()} and \code{summary()} methods describe the data
without echoing any adjacency matrix.

<<two-group, prompt=TRUE>>=
library("BrainNetTest")

group_a <- generate_category_graphs(
  n_graphs = 20, n_nodes = 10, n_communities = 2,
  base_intra_prob = 0.80, base_inter_prob = 0.10, seed = 1
)
group_b <- generate_category_graphs(
  n_graphs = 20, n_nodes = 10, n_communities = 2,
  base_intra_prob = 0.45, base_inter_prob = 0.45, seed = 2
)

networks <- brainnet_data(list(GroupA = group_a, GroupB = group_b))
networks
summary(networks)
@

Passing the validated object to \code{brainnet_test()} runs both inferential
layers and returns a classed \class{brainnet\_result}.

<<two-group-test, prompt=TRUE>>=
result <- brainnet_test(
  networks, n_permutations = 999, adjust = "holm", seed = 42
)
result
head(selected_edges(result)[, c(
  "node1", "node2", "risk_difference", "p_adjusted"
)])
@

The printed result does not dump stored matrices. \code{as.data.frame(result)}
returns the complete edge table, \code{summary(result)} adds the highest-ranked
edges, and \code{plot(result)} draws the classed result (Figure~\ref{fig:plot}
uses the three-group result below).

\subsection{Three groups}

Multi-group input uses the same constructor and method dispatch. Edge p-values
are omnibus; effects are observed ranges.

<<three-group, prompt=TRUE>>=
group_c <- generate_category_graphs(
  n_graphs = 20, n_nodes = 10, n_communities = 2,
  base_intra_prob = 0.25, base_inter_prob = 0.65, seed = 3
)
three_groups <- brainnet_data(
  list(GroupA = group_a, GroupB = group_b, GroupC = group_c)
)
three_result <- brainnet_test(
  three_groups, n_permutations = 999, adjust = "holm", seed = 43
)
three_result
@

The \code{plot()} method reuses the central graphs and selected edges stored in
the result, so the original populations need not be supplied again
(Figure~\ref{fig:plot}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
<<plot, echo=TRUE, fig.width=7, fig.height=7, out.width="0.75\\textwidth">>=
plot(three_result, reference = "GroupC")
@
\caption{The \code{plot()} method applied to the three-group result. The first
three panels show each group central graph, with edge width encoding the
empirical edge frequency; the final panel highlights the
multiplicity-controlled selected edges in red over the \code{GroupC}
central graph.}
\label{fig:plot}
\end{figure}
\FloatBarrier

\section{Clinical application: Autism and resting-state connectivity}
\label{sec:application}

Autism spectrum disorder has been associated with altered resting-state
functional connectivity, but findings can be distributed across many
connections and are sensitive to site, motion, preprocessing, and
thresholding \citep{DiMartino2014}. We use the openly shared, preprocessed
resting-state fMRI derivatives of ABIDE~I
\citep{DiMartino2014,Craddock2013} to ask a clinically motivated question:
does diagnosis group correspond to a different spatial pattern of binary
functional connections?

To avoid combining scanner sites, we use only the NYU sample and retain
participants with mean framewise displacement below 0.2. For each participant
we take mean time series from the AAL atlas
\citep{TzourioMazoyer2002}, form the Pearson correlation matrix, and keep the
strongest 10\% of edges. Proportional thresholding gives every network the
same edge count and avoids density confounding \citep{vandenHeuvel2017};
therefore the test concerns the anatomical distribution of edges, not total
network density. The standalone \code{data-application.R} script downloads
the public derivatives and records every construction choice.

<<abide-load, prompt=TRUE>>=
connectomes <- readRDS("application/abide_connectomes.rds")
abide_full <- brainnet_data(
  connectomes$populations,
  node_labels = connectomes$node_labels
)
abide_balanced <- brainnet_data(
  connectomes$balanced_populations,
  node_labels = connectomes$node_labels
)
abide_full
abide_balanced
@

<<abide-test, prompt=TRUE>>=
abide_full_result <- brainnet_test(
  abide_full, n_permutations = 999, adjust = "holm", seed = 42
)
abide_balanced_result <- brainnet_test(
  abide_balanced, n_permutations = 999, adjust = "holm", seed = 42
)
abide_full_result
abide_balanced_result
@

<<abide-numbers, echo=FALSE>>=
subjects <- read.csv("application/abide_subjects.csv")
balanced_subjects <- subjects[subjects$balanced, ]
balance <- aggregate(
  cbind(AGE_AT_SCAN, func_mean_fd) ~ group,
  balanced_subjects,
  mean
)
abide_edges <- as.data.frame(abide_balanced_result)
abide_edges$p_by <- stats::p.adjust(abide_edges$p_value, method = "BY")
n_control <- unname(abide_full_result$group_sizes["control"])
n_autism <- unname(abide_full_result$group_sizes["autism"])
n_balanced <- unname(abide_balanced_result$group_sizes["control"])
full_T <- unname(abide_full_result$global$statistic)
full_p <- abide_full_result$global$p_value
balanced_T <- unname(abide_balanced_result$global$statistic)
balanced_p <- abide_balanced_result$global$p_value
n_raw_strong <- sum(abide_edges$p_value < 0.001)
n_holm <- sum(abide_balanced_result$edges$selected)
n_by <- sum(abide_edges$p_by <= 0.05)
min_adjusted <- min(abide_edges$p_adjusted)
mean_age_autism <- balance$AGE_AT_SCAN[balance$group == "autism"]
mean_age_control <- balance$AGE_AT_SCAN[balance$group == "control"]
mean_fd_autism <- balance$func_mean_fd[balance$group == "autism"]
mean_fd_control <- balance$func_mean_fd[balance$group == "control"]
@

The full cohort contains \Sexpr{n_control} control and \Sexpr{n_autism} autism
participants. Sex composition and mean motion differ between these
observational groups, so we also construct a deterministic 1:1 coarsened
balance sample without using any network outcome: exact sex strata, five-year
age bins, and 0.05 mean-displacement bins. This retains \Sexpr{n_balanced}
participants per group (49 male and 6 female in each); mean ages are
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", mean_age_control)} and
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.2f", mean_age_autism)} years, and mean framewise
displacements are \Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", mean_fd_control)} and
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", mean_fd_autism)}.

The observed whole-graph statistic is extreme under label randomization in the
full cohort
($T=\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", full_T)}$,
$p=\Sexpr{format.pval(full_p, digits = 2)}$) and in the balanced sensitivity
sample ($T=\Sexpr{sprintf("%.1f", balanced_T)}$,
$p=\Sexpr{format.pval(balanced_p, digits = 2)}$). Thus, conditional on this
site, preprocessing pipeline, atlas, and threshold, the data show a
population-level association between diagnosis label and the spatial pattern
of resting-state connections. Balancing the measured sex, age, and motion
distributions does not remove that association. The balanced analysis of
\Sexpr{2 * n_balanced} networks, 114 regions,
\Sexpr{abide_balanced_result$edge_inference$family_size} edge hypotheses, and
999 random assignments completes in under one second on the reference system.

Edge localization is more demanding. In the balanced sample,
\Sexpr{n_raw_strong} edges have raw p-values below 0.001, but none survives
Holm family-wise control or BY false-discovery control at $\alpha=0.05$; the
smallest Holm-adjusted p-value is
\Sexpr{sprintf("%.3f", min_adjusted)}. The strongest observed differences are
reported for anatomical context, not as selected clinical biomarkers.
Figure~\ref{fig:abide} displays their anatomical distribution and edge-wise
evidence.

<<abide-edges, prompt=TRUE>>=
edges <- as.data.frame(abide_balanced_result)
edges$region1 <- connectomes$node_labels[edges$node1]
edges$region2 <- connectomes$node_labels[edges$node2]
top_edges <- head(edges[order(edges$p_value), c(
  "region1", "region2", "risk_difference", "p_adjusted"
)], 5)
rownames(top_edges) <- NULL
top_edges
@

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
<<abide-clinical, echo=FALSE, fig.width=10, fig.height=4.6, out.width="\\textwidth">>=
region_system <- function(label) {
  if (grepl("Cerebelum|Vermis", label)) return("Cerebellar")
  if (grepl("Caudate|Putamen|Pallidum|Thalamus", label)) return("Subcortical")
  if (grepl("Heschl|Temporal", label)) return("Temporal")
  if (grepl("Postcentral|Parietal|SupraMarginal|Angular|Precuneus|Paracentral",
            label)) return("Parietal")
  if (grepl("Calcarine|Cuneus|Lingual|Occipital|Fusiform", label)) {
    return("Occipital")
  }
  if (grepl("Hippocampus|ParaHippocampal|Amygdala", label)) return("Limbic")
  if (grepl("Cingulum", label)) return("Cingulate")
  if (grepl("Insula", label)) return("Insula")
  "Frontal"
}
systems <- vapply(connectomes$node_labels, region_system, character(1L))
system_levels <- c(
  "Frontal", "Insula", "Cingulate", "Limbic", "Occipital",
  "Parietal", "Subcortical", "Temporal", "Cerebellar"
)
systems <- factor(systems, levels = system_levels)
node_order <- order(systems, connectomes$node_labels)
system_code <- as.integer(systems[node_order])
system_runs <- rle(system_code)
system_ends <- cumsum(system_runs$lengths)
system_starts <- c(1L, head(system_ends, -1L) + 1L)
system_midpoints <- (system_starts + system_ends) / 2
system_label_map <- c(
  Frontal = "Front", Insula = "Ins", Cingulate = "Cing",
  Limbic = "Limb", Occipital = "Occ", Parietal = "Par",
  Subcortical = "Sub", Temporal = "Temp", Cerebellar = "Cer"
)
system_labels <- unname(
  system_label_map[system_levels[system_runs$values]]
)
control_central <- central_graph(
  connectomes$balanced_populations$control
)
autism_central <- central_graph(
  connectomes$balanced_populations$autism
)
frequency_difference <- autism_central - control_central
frequency_difference <- frequency_difference[node_order, node_order]
color_limit <- max(abs(frequency_difference))
colors <- grDevices::hcl.colors(41L, "Blue-Red 3")
colors[is.na(colors)] <- "#FFFFFF"
breaks <- seq(-color_limit, color_limit, length.out = length(colors) + 1L)

op <- graphics::par(mfrow = c(1, 2), mar = c(5.2, 5.2, 2, 1))
graphics::image(
  seq_len(nrow(frequency_difference)),
  seq_len(ncol(frequency_difference)),
  frequency_difference,
  col = colors,
  breaks = breaks,
  useRaster = TRUE,
  axes = FALSE,
  xlab = "AAL anatomical system",
  ylab = "AAL anatomical system",
  main = "A: Edge-frequency differences"
)
graphics::axis(1, at = system_midpoints, labels = system_labels, las = 2)
graphics::axis(2, at = system_midpoints, labels = system_labels, las = 2)
graphics::abline(
  v = head(system_ends, -1L) + 0.5,
  h = head(system_ends, -1L) + 0.5,
  col = "grey75",
  lwd = 0.6
)
graphics::box()
plot(
  abide_edges$risk_difference, -log10(abide_edges$p_value),
  pch = 16, cex = 0.5, col = grDevices::adjustcolor("steelblue", 0.5),
  xlab = "Edge frequency difference (autism minus control)",
  ylab = expression(-log[10] ~ italic(p) ~ (raw)),
  main = "B: Edge-wise tests"
)
graphics::abline(v = 0, lty = 3, col = "grey50")
top <- order(abide_edges$p_value)[seq_len(10)]
graphics::points(
  abide_edges$risk_difference[top], -log10(abide_edges$p_value[top]),
  col = "firebrick", cex = 1.1
)
graphics::par(op)
@
\caption{ABIDE clinical-group results in the balanced sensitivity sample.
Panel A shows autism-minus-control differences in empirical edge frequency
(blue: less frequent in autism; red: more frequent), with AAL regions grouped
into broad anatomical systems. Panel B shows the same
\Sexpr{abide_balanced_result$edge_inference$family_size} edges by effect and
raw significance; the ten smallest raw p-values are circled. The global test
detects a change in the distributed edge pattern, but no individual edge
survives Holm or BY control.}
\label{fig:abide}
\end{figure}
\FloatBarrier

This real-world result is clinically relevant but not diagnostic or causal.
It is consistent with a distributed change in functional network organization
for this NYU sample, while strict edge-level control prevents unsupported
claims about single connections. Unmeasured confounding, the selected
preprocessing pipeline, atlas, and threshold remain possible explanations.
The application therefore shows both the value and the limits of the package:
it reports a preprocessing-conditional population association and states
clearly when localization is not supported.

\section{Prespecified simulation validation}
\label{sec:validation}

The inferential simulation cells and pass/fail criteria were frozen before the
full run and are distributed in \code{simulation-design.md}; the separately
disclosed prefix-sum timing benchmark was added during the brain-first
manuscript restructuring and does not alter these cells. Every complete-null
cell has
\CompleteNullReps{} replications; each partial-null cell has
\PartialNullReps{}. The complete-null gate required the one-sided 95\%
Wilson upper limit for both global rejection and Holm family-wise error to be
at most 0.065. This is an implementation tolerance, not a proof of exactness.

\subsection{Complete null}

The six cells include independent edges, unequal sample sizes, positive
within-graph dependence from latent density, negative dependence from fixed
density, and sparse/dense three-group settings. Observations remain
independent between subjects. Table~\ref{tab:complete-null} and
Figure~\ref{fig:complete-null} summarize the calibration results.

<<complete-table, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
complete <- read.csv("generated/tables/complete_null_summary.csv")
complete <- complete[, c(
  "scenario", "global_rejection", "global_upper_95",
  "holm_fwer", "holm_upper_95", "by_fdr"
)]
latex_table(
  complete,
  "Complete-null calibration. Upper limits are one-sided 95\\% Wilson bounds.",
  "tab:complete-null"
)
@

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
  {generated/figures/complete_null_calibration.pdf}
\caption{Global rejection and Holm family-wise error under the complete null.
Vertical segments end at the prespecified one-sided 95\% upper bounds; the
dashed line is $\alpha=0.05$.}
\label{fig:complete-null}
\end{figure}
\FloatBarrier

\subsection{Partial nulls and localization}

Four alternatives cover localized positive shifts, scattered mixed-direction
shifts, latent-density dependence among null edges, and three groups.
Table~\ref{tab:partial-null} reports unconditional results, and
Figure~\ref{fig:localization} compares localization rates; the separately
labeled conditional table is included in the replication material.
If \(R\) is the number selected, precision and false-discovery proportion
divide by \(\max(R,1)\), so both are zero when no edge is selected.

<<partial-table, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
partial <- read.csv("generated/tables/partial_null_summary.csv")
partial <- partial[
  partial$procedure %in% c("Holm", "BY"),
  c("scenario", "procedure", "global_power", "tpr", "precision",
    "fdp", "fwer", "jaccard", "mean_selected")
]
latex_table(
  partial,
  "Unconditional global power and edge-localization performance.",
  "tab:partial-null"
)
@

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]
  {generated/figures/localization_performance.pdf}
\caption{Mean true-positive rate and precision for Holm and BY selections
across the four partial-null alternatives.}
\label{fig:localization}
\end{figure}

<<partial-prose, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
partial_metrics <- read.csv("generated/tables/partial_null_summary.csv")
holm <- partial_metrics[partial_metrics$procedure == "Holm", ]
unadjusted <- partial_metrics[partial_metrics$procedure == "unadjusted", ]
cat(sprintf(
  paste(
    "Global power ranged from %.3f to %.3f.",
    "Holm true-positive rate ranged from %.3f to %.3f, while precision",
    "ranged from %.3f to %.3f and family-wise error from %.3f to %.3f.",
    "The unadjusted comparator increased true-positive rate to %.3f--%.3f",
    "but raised family-wise error to %.3f--%.3f.",
    "Thus multiplicity control is effective but materially conservative",
    "at 15--20 observations per group.\n\n"
  ),
  min(holm$global_power), max(holm$global_power),
  min(holm$tpr), max(holm$tpr),
  min(holm$precision), max(holm$precision),
  min(holm$fwer), max(holm$fwer),
  min(unadjusted$tpr), max(unadjusted$tpr),
  min(unadjusted$fwer), max(unadjusted$fwer)
))
@
\FloatBarrier

\subsection{Scope stress test and sensitivity}

The weak-null stress cell gives both groups marginal edge probability 0.5 but
uses perfectly dependent edges in one group and independent edges in the
other. Whole-graph exchangeability is false, so its global rejection rate is
not a Type-I error estimate for the package's global null. Every edge-marginal
null is true, however, so Holm family-wise error remains a required check.
This cell makes the distinction between the two inferential layers explicit.

<<weak-prose, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
weak <- read.csv("generated/tables/weak_null_summary.csv")
cat(sprintf(
  paste(
    "Observed global rejection was %.3f; this is descriptive because the",
    "whole-graph null is false. Holm family-wise error was %.3f, with",
    "one-sided 95\\%% upper bound %.3f.\n\n"
  ),
  weak$global_rejection,
  weak$holm_fwer,
  weak$holm_upper_95
))
@

Sensitivity runs compare 99, 199, 999, and 4,999 random assignments on common
data. Edge decisions are invariant to this setting because edge inference is
separate from global randomization; global p-value resolution and elapsed time
change as expected. Full cell-level results are in the replication tables.

<<sensitivity-prose, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
sensitivity <- read.csv("generated/tables/sensitivity_summary.csv")
cat(sprintf(
  paste(
    "Global power was %.3f with 99 assignments and %.3f with 4,999;",
    "the mean number of Holm-selected edges remained %.3f in every row.",
    "Median runtime rose from %.3f to %.3f seconds.\n\n"
  ),
  sensitivity$global_power[1L],
  sensitivity$global_power[nrow(sensitivity)],
  sensitivity$mean_selected[1L],
  sensitivity$median_seconds[1L],
  sensitivity$median_seconds[nrow(sensitivity)]
))
@

\FloatBarrier
\section{Computational performance}
\label{sec:performance}

\subsection{Chunked global randomization}

The benchmark compares the chunked matrix engine with a direct loop over the
same 499 assignment matrix. Equality of every assignment score is checked
before timing is accepted. Each method is warmed up, measurement order is
randomized, and fast calls are timed in calibrated batches.
Table~\ref{tab:benchmark} reports medians over 10 repetitions; the largest measured
median speedup was
\LargestMeasuredSpeedup{}. Figure~\ref{fig:performance} shows scaling over the
measured edge range.

<<benchmark-table, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
benchmark <- read.csv("generated/tables/benchmark_summary.csv")
latex_table(
  benchmark,
  "Measured global-engine runtime and input-object size summaries.",
  "tab:benchmark",
  digits = 4L
)
@

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]
  {generated/figures/performance.pdf}
\caption{Measured runtime for direct and chunked assignment-score
implementations. Both axes are logarithmic.}
\label{fig:performance}
\end{figure}
\FloatBarrier

\subsection{Prefix-sum ablation updates}

The second benchmark isolates the ranked-removal update optimized by the
prefix-sum algorithm. For each of 99 fixed assignment rows, both
implementations receive the same edge-contribution vector and fixed edge
order. The direct implementation repeatedly sums every remaining tail; the
optimized implementation obtains all residual scores by one cumulative sum.
Every complete path is checked for numerical equality before timing.
Table~\ref{tab:ablation-benchmark} and
Figure~\ref{fig:ablation-performance} report the update-stage results.

<<ablation-benchmark-table, echo=FALSE, results='asis'>>=
ablation_benchmark <- read.csv(
  "generated/tables/ablation_benchmark_summary.csv"
)
latex_table(
  ablation_benchmark,
  paste(
    "Measured runtime for repeated-tail and prefix-sum ablation updates.",
    "The contribution matrix is computed before both timings."
  ),
  "tab:ablation-benchmark",
  digits = 5L
)
@

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.75\textwidth]
  {generated/figures/ablation_performance.pdf}
\caption{Measured runtime of the ablation path-update stage. Both methods
produce identical residual paths; both axes are logarithmic.}
\label{fig:ablation-performance}
\end{figure}
\FloatBarrier

Across the measured range, the largest median update-stage speedup was
\LargestAblationSpeedup{}. This is deliberately not reported as an end-to-end
speedup: edge ranking and the assignment-by-edge contribution calculation are
common to both methods. Together, the two benchmarks show that the software
engineering contribution covers both the main randomization engine and the
iterative diagnostic inherited from the original package.

The benchmarks do not extrapolate unobserved multi-day runtimes. Performance
depends on hardware, BLAS, graph density, and the chosen edge test; full
session information and raw timings are distributed.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\pkg{BrainNetTest}~1.0.0 turns the Fraiman and Fraiman brain-network test into
a validated clinical connectomics workflow. The classed interface makes
malformed connectome populations fail early, standard methods make results
inspectable without list plumbing, and the result records the randomization
and multiplicity information needed for interpretation. Global inference,
edge-level inference, and adaptive ablation now have distinct names and
contracts.

The ABIDE application demonstrates real-world use rather than only a simulated
example. In both the full cohort and the sex-, age-, and motion-balanced
sensitivity sample, the observed global statistic is extreme under
autism-control label randomization. No individual edge survives strict
multiplicity control. Clinically, this is a useful distinction: the data
support a preprocessing-conditional population association, but they do not
support a single-edge biomarker claim. The result is not causal or diagnostic,
and it remains tied to the selected sample, preprocessing pipeline, atlas, and
threshold.

The computational contribution is also retained. Chunked matrix products
accelerate randomization scores, while exact prefix-sum updates replace
repeated edge-removal calculations. These are software-engineering
improvements to a statistical workflow, not substitutes for inferential
validation; Section~\ref{sec:performance} therefore reports both numerical
identity and measured speed.

The supported domain remains precise: aligned binary undirected brain networks
from independent participants. The global score is sensitive to marginal
edge-frequency patterns rather than every possible distributional change.
Holm control can be conservative when thousands of connections are tested,
while BY trades additional conservatism for false-discovery control under
general dependence. The same mathematical contract can be useful outside
neuroimaging, but brain-network population analysis is the package's primary
application.

Future work should add restricted randomization for family, site, paired, and
longitudinal designs; covariate-adjusted edge models; principled support for
weighted or directed networks; and external validation across clinical
cohorts. Such extensions require new contracts and validation rather than
coercion inside the current API.

\section*{Computational details}

The package requires \proglang{R}~4.0.0 or later and imports \pkg{stats},
\pkg{graphics}, and \pkg{igraph} \citep{igraph}. Unit tests use \pkg{testthat}
\citep{testthat}; the vignettes and this manuscript are built with \pkg{knitr}
\citep{knitr} and \pkg{rmarkdown} \citep{rmarkdown}. The authoritative
\code{code-full.R} script regenerates every reported simulation and benchmark
result, table, figure, and numeric macro. The \code{data-application.R} script
downloads the openly shared ABIDE derivatives used in
Section~\ref{sec:application} and saves the derived binary connectomes that the
manuscript reads; it is the only component that needs network access.
A separate standalone \code{code.R} and generated \code{code.html} exercise a
reduced pipeline within the reviewer-time guideline. Full
\code{sessionInfo()} output is supplied in
\code{generated/sessionInfo.txt}.

